      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to use the concept of independent probabilities. Since the probability of rain on Saturday and the probability of rain on Sunday are independent, the probability that it will rain on both days is the product of the individual probabilities.\n\nGiven:\n- The probability it will rain on Saturday is \\( P(S) = 60\\% = 0.60 \\).\n- The probability it will rain on Sunday is \\( P(U) = 25\\% = 0.25 \\).\n\nThe probability that it will rain on both days is:\n\\[ P(S \\text{ and } U) = P(S) \\times P(U) = 0.60 \\times 0.25 \\]\n\nLet's calculate this using Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Given probabilities\nP_S = 0.60  # Probability of rain on Saturday\nP_U = 0.25  # Probability of rain on Sunday\n\n# Probability of rain on both days\nP_S_and_U = P_S * P_U\nP_S_and_U_percent = P_S_and_U * 100  # Convert to percent\n\nprint(P_S_and_U_percent)\n```\n```output\n15.0\n```\nThe probability that it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday is \\(\\boxed{15\\%}\\).",
