      "text": "To determine the probability that it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday, we need to use the fact that the probability of independent events occurring together is the product of their individual probabilities.\n\n1. Identify the probability that it will rain on Saturday.\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{Rain on Saturday}) = 0.60\n   \\]\n\n2. Identify the probability that it will rain on Sunday.\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{Rain on Sunday}) = 0.25\n   \\]\n\n3. Since the events are independent, the probability that it will rain on both days is the product of the probabilities of rain on each day.\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{Rain on both days}) = P(\\text{Rain on Saturday}) \\times P(\\text{Rain on Sunday}) = 0.60 \\times 0.25\n   \\]\n\n4. Calculate the product.\n   \\[\n   0.60 \\times 0.25 = 0.15\n   \\]\n\n5. Convert the probability to a percent.\n   \\[\n   0.15 = 15\\%\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday is \\(\\boxed{15}\\).",
